<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Nasrid Maliks of Sistan</h1><p class="st-meta">1029 CE – 1225 CE (approx.) · capital Zaranj</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Descendants of the Saffarids who ruled Sistan as hereditary maliks under a succession of overlords for two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a> absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, a branch of the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarid dynasty</a> regained local power. In 1029 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-fadl-nasr-ibn-ahmad">Abu'l-Fadl Nasr ibn Ahmad</a> established himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaranj">Zaranj</a>, founding a line of maliks often called the Nasrids or Maliks of Nimruz.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The maliks governed the irrigated delta of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmand-river">Helmand</a> as local hereditary rulers, accepting the overlordship in turn of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavids</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>. The anonymous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-i-sistan">Tarikh-i Sistan</a>, a Persian local history, reflects their court's pride in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan's</a> past, from the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rustam</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-al-din-harb">Taj al-Din Harb</a>, who reigned for nearly fifty years until 1215, was the most notable of the line.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Mongol invasion devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> in the early 1220s, and the last Nasrid rulers were killed around 1225. Local rule soon passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mihrabanid-dynasty">Mihrabanid dynasty</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1029 CE – 1225 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:80.18%;width:3.90%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1029 CE – 1225 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zaranj</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu'l-Fadl Nasr ibn Ahmad, Taj al-Din Harb</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maliks of Nimruz, Nasrids of Sistan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1127&amp;focus=nasrid-maliks-of-sistan" aria-label="Nasrid Maliks of Sistan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6280 1049 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nasrid Maliks of Sistan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6600 1305L6600 1305M6600 1305L6481 1127M6600 1305L6480 1127"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6600 1305h0M6481 1127h0M6480 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6600 1305h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6600 1305h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks 